Java Code:
class Person {
    String name;
    int id;
    void displayDetails() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name + ", ID: " + id);
    }
}
class Teacher extends Person {
    String subject;
    void teach() {
        System.out.println(name + " teaches " + subject);
    }
}
class Student extends Person {
    int grade;
    void study() {
        System.out.println(name + " studies in grade " + grade);
    }
}
public class SchoolSystem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Teacher t = new Teacher();
        t.name = "Mr. Ravi";
        t.id = 101;
        t.subject = "Math";
        t.displayDetails();
        t.teach();
        Student s = new Student();
        s.name = "Asha";
        s.id = 202;
        s.grade = 10;
        s.displayDetails();
        s.study();
    }
}
Explanation:
The base class Person holds common properties: name and id. Teacher and Student are subclasses, adding role-specific behavior (teach() and study()). This is an example of hierarchical inheritance, where multiple subclasses inherit from a single superclass.
Benefits:
Promotes code reuse by avoiding duplication of common properties.
Supports scalability�more roles like Admin, Counselor can be added easily.
Enhances clarity and maintainability by modeling real-world relationships.

